{"title":"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>An integer is called snakelike if its decimal representation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/f/a5f78bb05961b9a37e45022ced3d254889f7e710.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1a_2a_3\\cdots a_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"13\" /> satisfies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/5/7f5cb889dabcb8976c412b74bd50b7aa78c1521b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i&lt;a_{i+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"14\" /> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> is <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Odd_integer\" title=\"Odd integer\"> odd</a> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/4/68488324270214657b670eb5763d5e7b69fc000c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i&gt;a_{i+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"14\" /> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> is <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Even_integer\" title=\"Even integer\"> even</a>. How many snakelike integers between 1000 and 9999 have four distinct digits?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We divide the problem into two cases: one in which zero is not one of the digits and one in which it is. In the former case, suppose we pick digits \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/9/df93ae0e9ebaf28a0afa5a27f3475c7f84baea9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"11\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/7/4a7180177440903e39f77cf7cb8ac3ec1e13da8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1&lt;x_2&lt;x_3&lt;x_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"143\" height=\"12\" />. There are five arrangements of these digits that satisfy the condition of being snakelike: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/2/9026c871481bb3fd54690d4adf22ee3da00a6138.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1x_3x_2x_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"70\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/4/814e85a7289e34e8925c37e2f520a327d0e37b61.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1x_4x_2x_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"70\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/c/8ac6af201040e531ad5fecc0a5fb1f30a364e5ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_2x_3x_1x_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"70\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/2/1f217a34c2b809c36cb629aee94d2dedff09fa98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_2x_4x_1x_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"70\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/d/a9daa1d92d42f397394dfe28243c7270371670ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_3x_4x_1x_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"13\" />. Thus there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/d/76de82e80c10d6c136c25f3267e58faf23585702.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5\\cdot {9\\choose 4}=630$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"110\" height=\"53\" /> snakelike numbers which do not contain the digit zero.\n</p><p>In the second case we choose zero and three other digits such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/3/6e3bf3ebb4e537abbe6c0a7d02ebbfdfa109353e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;x_2&lt;x_3&lt;x_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"134\" height=\"15\" />. There are three arrangements of these digits that satisfy the condition of being snakelike: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/0/370242dba09c9c9294c54694d5f8b5c05d533fea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_2x_30x_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"61\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/acc93a070c05367923972cae194604922d1f8a3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_2x_40x_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"17\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/1/651dbbb1b4ad84066335a2398f30142e87d31e74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_3x_40x_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"61\" height=\"15\" />. Because we know that zero is a digit, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/f/d2fedfa4271826fc66d0a4e9b62c0b8af2308db0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\cdot{9\\choose 3}=252$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"110\" height=\"53\" /> snakelike numbers which contain the digit zero. Thus there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/9/bd9e4fdda6a94a19d6c16728e1d458e8b5c11ed1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$630+252=\\boxed{882}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"140\" height=\"23\" /> snakelike numbers.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let's create the snakelike number from digits <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/a/79aaead4a185db1b2c9c7ccf238bd329165edfc4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a &lt; b &lt; c &lt; d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"109\" height=\"15\" />, and, if we already picked the digits there are 5 ways to do so, as said in the first solution. And, let's just pick the digits from 0-9. This get's a total count of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/0/c8034f26d9e288de2d65be59934f02756f2cacc2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5\\cdot{10 \\choose 4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"70\" height=\"55\" /> But, this over-counts since it counts numbers like 0213. We can correct for this over-counting. Lock the first digit as 0 and permute 3 other chosen digits <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/2/7c286a6b2750b9c5c514b55b7f52660b1f66c21f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a &lt; b &lt; c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" />. There are 2 ways to permute to make the number snakelike, b-a-c, or c-a-b. And, we pick a,b,c from 1 to 9, since 0 has already been chosen as one of the digits. So, the amount we have overcounted by is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/c/64c484616f516c76c937b446f48b297284e32869.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\cdot{9 \\choose 3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"55\" height=\"53\" />. Thus our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/0/a50e05010726eb6feedb58bc10815f2022cf1bd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5\\cdot{10 \\choose 4} - 2\\cdot{9 \\choose 3} = \\boxed{882}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"215\" height=\"55\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We will first decide the order of the 4 digits, greatest to least. To do this, we will pretend that we have selected the digits 1,2,3,4, and we need to arrange them to create a snakelike number. By testing all permutations, there are only 5 ways to make a snakelike number: (1,3,2,4),(1,4,2,3),(2,3,1,4),(2,4,1,3),(3,4,1,2). \n</p><p>Now we select 4 digits to replace the 1,2,3,4. \n</p><p>In first 2 of cases: (1,3,2,4),(1,4,2,3), the leading digit is a 1, which means it is the smallest of our 4 digits. If we select a 0, the leading digit will be a zero, which is bad because all numbers between 1000 and 9999 have nonzero leading digits. So, we need to select our 4 digits only from the pool of 1-9. There are 9 choose 4 ways and there are 2 cases:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/8/6e8f9aa1be529a85cad8c1705b8e0d9acd37f866.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\cdot \\dbinom{9}{4} = 252$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"110\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>Thus, there are 252 ways for those 2 cases. \n</p><p>For the next 3 cases, selecting a 0 is okay, so we can select from the pool of 0-9. There are 10 choose 4 ways to select our 4 digits and there are 3 cases:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/1/e21ad65b6b4497f40925f9d8ef26a936e8f08eb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\cdot \\dbinom{10}{4} = 630$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"119\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>For those 3 cases there are 630 ways. \n</p><p>Thus, our answer is 630+252 = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/a/05ae9291b4dc217f3e7044cc6c9b59ccfd6c4637.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{882}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p><p>-Alexlikemath\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I\" title=\"2004 AIME I\">2004 AIME I</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Answer_Key\" title=\"2004 AIME I Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2004\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 7\">Problem 7</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2004 AIME I Problems/Problem 15\">15</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AIME_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AIME Problems and Solutions\"> All AIME Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017032236\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.033 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.052 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 218/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1786/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 108/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5066/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   12.242      1 -total\n 57.92%    7.090      1 Template:AIME_box\n 40.33%    4.937      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:2686:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017032236 and revision id 289025. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}